#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495258} :activity/id #uuid "f7834130-0260-5c43-bf45-c2988a6f854e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049849 :topic/id #uuid "1887f465-a1b8-5370-82f6-80e02b9d153a" :topic/math-academy-id 3120} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495205 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495170 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a866929-0beb-5a32-8959-34966c6e6ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494370} {:answer/id #uuid "28cc6daa-99e2-5439-a52d-8c74f6f8d55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494393} {:answer/id #uuid "7d73673d-5635-5b93-8b05-960db3e3f68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494590} {:answer/id #uuid "891a910a-dee7-5f7a-b066-db75538859fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494623} {:answer/id #uuid "fec25ec0-ae75-52d4-992b-cbf821a92df5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494915}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494915} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f1f6569-2671-58ca-aa36-0fefdf95c894" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f8b0f486-8442-538c-933a-d85ad556ff51" :question/math-academy-id "q-95716" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$ Computing $A-1I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 17\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{4}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=3,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=5.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}16 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "67e20c48-efe2-5fd4-8d06-cb1125f84345" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495243}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495243 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378065 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddb77fdb-9e1a-59d2-a520-e52d80297bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378067} {:answer/id #uuid "20a24a23-34cd-59e2-919c-ffa350d8078c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378068} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f8cd17-d0e2-5147-8618-30de79a463fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378069} {:answer/id #uuid "5649503f-0459-5e9f-9fcf-d4672d0737b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378070} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6a63c9-7482-5e58-94cf-dffcb3862ae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378069} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ed15ba3-7536-51e1-aa4c-32ec9da0175e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e3527660-7336-5854-9fe8-3ed70b1dd1cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-95723" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}(5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}]+(-5)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-40 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 40\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b38fd098-3465-5c13-8a12-93f1b35b5090" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495260}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162662 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07faab27-15f7-5312-8b38-59ad02f72521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494338} {:answer/id #uuid "2f44ec68-5673-5d17-a202-807989a0d5ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494410} {:answer/id #uuid "6c89eafa-421f-5962-9af4-946ba03a1964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494540} {:answer/id #uuid "8b205150-ffac-59f3-b8f8-30947cc22faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494635} {:answer/id #uuid "9d276ed0-9aab-5f35-92de-cd40b3be996e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494678}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494678} :answer-field/id #uuid "27a49c53-38b7-5dbb-b5f4-f47add37c5d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186161264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "28c27a06-cd3b-5e3d-a19e-4b46b9bccd18" :question/math-academy-id "q-95714" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection matrix that maps $x\\in {R}^{2}$ onto the subspace spanned by an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nMoreover, ${u}_{i}\\cdot {u}_{i}^{T}$ is the projection matrix corresponding to ${u}_{i}.$\n\nFirst, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda =-10.$ Computing $A-(-10)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+10I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 15\\end{aligned}]+10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 25\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 25 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-5{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖v‖=\\sqrt{26}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we obtain the normalized eigenvector: \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the required orthogonal projection matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {u}^{T} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{26}}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{26}[\\begin{aligned}25 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd9e99be-c8ca-50d9-aacd-09344ae9dae2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495205}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495258 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162647 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9efaaf0-779f-5a72-aa61-4761f6295dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366582} {:answer/id #uuid "5d20e65d-8c96-5a7e-8fea-9024d97dc82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366583} {:answer/id #uuid "d2283d99-cd32-55e5-9365-da3163600171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366584} {:answer/id #uuid "1172809a-fec9-5ba9-a260-4a0f08543d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366585} {:answer/id #uuid "d89d3c9b-2928-5750-a596-9fb110e7f349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366586} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4531768-c85a-5927-b9ea-dd6e8018f412" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48eccd0e-cb0a-5561-99a4-875f56fc9de1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94769" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-8)\\cdot \\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(9\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-8)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}(9\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-8)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 16\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}[\\begin{aligned}136 & -68 \\\\ -68 & -119\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d7e437ea-03ba-522c-8c2f-e2da5dacae70" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495260 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395066 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d17ddb9-9738-5247-980d-6346f34ca3d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395068} {:answer/id #uuid "9112c274-741d-5926-a7e8-28550395e3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395069} {:answer/id #uuid "a6833e0f-d872-5f84-8fad-2cd587942a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395070} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a1eeb9-2422-526b-996e-3116b894cad2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395071} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3216f5-0141-54bf-b967-4b7d4a78081b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395072}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395068} :answer-field/id #uuid "da07241e-4465-5487-a804-a1f93208770d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4f741f54-e9ba-55b4-9c81-df73745d51c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95724" :question/problem "Suppose that\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nare eigenvectors of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ respectively. Find the matrix $A.$" :question/worked-solution "The spectral decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are orthonormal eigenvectors of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ respectively.\n\nFirst, let's normalize our eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\lambda }_{1}{u}_{1}{u}_{1}^{T}+{\\lambda }_{2}{u}_{2}{u}_{2}^{T} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc9cbb5e-30fb-5f9a-8c8a-71b1f6f7926c"}] :activity/title "The Spectral Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494318}]}
